Yes. I think **“sensors into reality for agents” is a genuinely useful framing**, and your Hermes annoyances are exactly the kind of pain cuntgoblin should mine.

The important inversion is:

> **Agents increasingly have enough intelligence. What they lack is dependable perception, execution and feedback from the outside world.**

A model can reason perfectly about a page and still fail because the page returns 403 from a VPS. It can know how to process a YouTube video but fail because the execution environment is a datacenter IP. It can correctly plan a 12-hour workflow and still lose everything because process #7 crashes. These are infrastructure problems rather than intelligence problems.

Your API Evangelist reference is especially relevant. Kin Lane's current definition of an API product emphasizes a known consumer, clear value, sustainable business model, roadmap and feedback loop—not merely exposing an endpoint. He has also started charging for deeper API/MCP access while keeping the public discovery layer open. That is very close to the model I'd use here. ([API Evangelist][1])

## First: what I mean by a “live fragmented-data API”

Imagine an agent wants to know:

> “What is the cheapest genuinely usable frontier-ish model for a 150k-context coding job **right now**?”

There is no authoritative endpoint containing that answer.

The evidence is scattered among provider pricing pages, promotions, docs, model cards, status pages, rate-limit documentation, OpenRouter, free tiers, Discord announcements, changelogs, benchmark providers and actual runtime testing.

That's **fragmented reality**.

Your product turns:

**100 messy changing sources**

into something like:

```text
GET /inference/options
  ?context_min=150000
  &task=coding
  &max_cost=0.50

→ model
→ provider
→ effective_price
→ promotional_price
→ promotion_expires_at
→ observed_latency
→ observed_throughput
→ context
→ tool_calling
→ rate_limit
→ signup_requirement
→ evidence[]
→ observed_at
→ confidence
```

And importantly:

```text
GET /changes?since=2026-08-17
```

The **change feed** may be more valuable than the database.

Exa is validating the generic layer of this thesis already: its Websets API searches, verifies, enriches and continuously monitors web-derived datasets, with webhook events as new information appears. ([Exa][2])

Your opportunity isn't “build another Exa.”

It is:

> **Use things like Exa/browser infrastructure underneath hundreds of opinionated vertical sensors.**

