# 2. `EgressMap` — “where can my agent actually execute this?”

Your `yt-dlp` issue is a fantastic seed.

The current yt-dlp project explicitly says that 403s can be expected on datacenter IP addresses, and its documentation tracks YouTube's newer Proof-of-Origin token requirements. There are recent 2026 reports specifically involving VPS/datacenter environments behaving differently from ordinary browser environments. ([GitHub][4])

Don't build:

> “YouTube-block bypass API.”

That's fragile and turns into an endless adversarial arms race.

Build the abstraction:

> **“This operation has environmental requirements. Where should it execute?”**

That's much broader.

Imagine:

```text
POST /capability/test

{
  "capability": "retrieve_video_metadata",
  "target": "youtube",
  "requirements": {
    "region": "GB"
  }
}
```

Or:

```text
GET /environments/best
  ?capability=playwright_login
```

Your agents could know:

```text
Hetzner Helsinki
  youtube_metadata       DEGRADED
  cloudflare_browser     N/A
  github                 OK
  huggingface            OK

User node / Cambodia
  youtube_metadata       OK
  government-site-X      OK

Browserbase US-West
  authenticated_browser  OK
  page-X                 OK
```

Now you've created **capability-aware compute routing**.

E2B validates one side of this market: agents increasingly get disposable, isolated computers/sandboxes, and its current agent integrations explicitly support persistent workspace state and parallel sandboxes. ([E2B][5])

But E2B answers:

> “Give me a computer.”

Your proposed system answers:

> **“Which computer/environment should this particular action occur on?”**

That's different.

I rate this **9/10**.

