# 6. Orchestration itself: don't build another CrewAI

This area is huge but crowded.

The real production problem has moved away from:

> “How do I get three agents to talk?”

toward:

> **“How do I guarantee that this 19-hour nondeterministic computation eventually finishes correctly without wasting everything when something dies?”**

Inngest now describes durable agents explicitly in these terms: each LLM/tool step is checkpointed, completed operations are memoized, agents can suspend without keeping a process alive, and failed substeps can retry independently. Trigger.dev likewise sells long-running execution, retries, queues, idempotency, human pauses and observability specifically for agents. ([Inngest][8])

So I would **not** make cuntgoblin generate:

`YetAnotherAgentFramework`

I would investigate gaps *above* these frameworks.

For example:

```text
Agent Supervisor
```

that can run Hermes, Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode or arbitrary shell agents and understand:

```text
alive
stuck
looping
productive
waiting
blocked
needs-human
budget-exceeded
completed
contradicting-another-agent
```

Then:

```text
restart
resume
fork
change-model
change-tool
escalate
kill
```

That is much closer to what you're already discovering with Hermes.

And importantly, there is research suggesting that elaborate orchestration can actually underperform simpler model-driven procedures for some procedural tasks, which reinforces the idea that the valuable layer isn't another fancy graph abstraction—it is **durability, measurement and supervision**. ([arXiv][9])

